Description:
  The Administering company banner and the Switch Company control.

  Shown only to a genuine platform administrator. An ordinary Company Administrator is pinned to
  their own company and never sees a switcher, which is why the whole block is guarded by the
  can_select_company capability rather than by a role name.

  The workspace always describes exactly one concrete company, so this states which one rather than
  offering an all-companies mode. The universe badge is part of that statement: administering a
  generated company looks identical to administering a real one until something says otherwise, and
  the company itself is what decides which universe the workspace is in.

  Switching opens the shared core modal. The first page of companies is rendered server-side inside
  it, so the control works with no JavaScript: the modal markup is still on the page, the search is
  an ordinary GET form and each result carries an ordinary POST. There is also a plain link to the
  standalone picker page for the same reason.

  Selection is a POST carrying a CSRF token, never a link and never a query parameter, because which
  company is being administered is an authorisation boundary rather than a display preference. The
  server re-validates the choice on every request and ignores anything it does not like, so this
  markup is a convenience and never the authority.

  WARNING: a template comment is not a safe place to document syntax. F3 parses its own tags
  inside comments, so a tag written out as a usage example becomes a real tag in the compiled
  template. A comment delimiter written inside a comment body ends it early and leaks the
  remainder onto the page. Keep every comment plain prose: no angle brackets, no comment
  delimiters, no template tokens and no code, not even to document one.
